A||||14||cmyokley||muffler||||
Z||000000||cmyokley||08-31-99||04:22 PM||||I have an 86GT.....a year ago I discarded the old worn out exhaust system. I bought a hook up that omited the muffler and added two resonator tips. I am now very tired of the loud exhaust and popping noise behind my head.<p>Does anyone knoe of a performance muffler source that will fit in the stock location?<p>Has anyone done anything creative with their exhaust system that they would like to share?<p>Any help would be appreciated.
Z||000001||batboy||08-31-99||05:47 PM||||A good muffler shop that does custom work should be able to weld in a number of different types of mufflers. For performance, you can't go wrong with Flowmasters, which is a turbo style muffler.
Z||000002||cmyokley||09-01-99||09:20 AM||||batboy,<p>I have tried two "custom" muffler shops. They both declined to do the work because it was tooooooooo hard. <p>The stock muffler for the GT has a 12" case length, that allows the pipe benders to get the pipe turned into the muffler. The Flowmaster has a 15" case and the quality pipe benders around here don't think it can be done. I don't agree. Someone has done this before. I cant believe that all the fieros in the world are running on the stock muffler system.<p>I have been toying with the idea of chopping 4" out of the bottom of my trunk and mounting a performance muffler under the trunk. Has anyone seen this done before? Any good reasons that this woul not work? 
Z||000003||Dale||09-01-99||10:29 AM||||It can be done.  They just want to charge you alot.  Another way to do some of the sharp bends is to find out what angles they are and order mandrel bent tubes.  flow charactoristics are much better with these.  You will still have to take it to an exhaust shop to have it welded up, but they can't bitch about the bends so much. <p> The flowmaster mufflers are as close to a free flow as you can get.  they use the princaple of sound cancelation using walls inside of the chamber to deflect sound waves as aposed to most mufflers which use perforated pipes.  If you have a Summit catalog there is a good picture of the internal structure.
Z||000004||batboy||09-01-99||04:20 PM||||I guess I'm lucky to have a good muffler shop near where I work. Jim, the manager, and I are on first name basis. He is damn good at custom bending. I tell him what I want and he does it, at a reasonable price too. Seems like I'm always taking one of my streetrods or cars into his shop. I say look for another muffler shop that wants your business and will work with you.<p>There are other brands of turbo mufflers that are smaller and may fit better. Might not last as long or flow as good as a Flowmaster, but would still better than stock or nothing.
Z||000005||cmyokley||09-02-99||05:25 AM||||Thanks guys, I think I will keep looking for someone willing to help.
Z||000006||batboy||09-02-99||06:21 AM||||Cmyokley, I have my handy dandy JEGS catalog in front of me and it shows Flowmaster 40 series performance mufflers that has a 13 inch long body with inlets/outlets ranging from 2, 2-1/4, 2-1/2, and 3 inch diameter. I'd think that would fit the Fiero pretty good. Price ranged from $55 to $62 each.
Z||000007||cmyokley||09-03-99||05:31 AM||||batbay,<p>Thanks...I looked up Jegs on the net and saw what you saw in your mag. I will look at that as a possibility. It should fit fine.<p>Thanks
